<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mac Giolla Phádraig</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as FitzPatrick</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mac Giolla Phádraig, later anglicised as FitzPatrick, was the ruling dynasty of the Irish kingdom of Osraige, named after Giolla Phádraig, a 10th-century king. After the Anglo-Norman invasion they lost the lowlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kilkenny">Kilkenny</a> but kept lordship in upper Osraige. In 1541 Barnaby Mac Giolla Phádraig surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> and was made Baron of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-ossory">Upper Ossory</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mac-giolla-phadraig.jpg" alt="Mac Giolla Phádraig" width="320" height="351" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wikimandia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fitzpatrick_of_Ossary.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name, in Old Irish Mac Gilla Pátraic, means son of the devotee of Patrick. Giolla Phádraig first appears in the annals at the end of the 10th century in connection with the Christianized Uí Ímair dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/waterford">Waterford</a>, and, probably through intermarriage, it came to be borne by the leading medieval branch of the Dál Birn, the ruling lineage of neighbouring Osraige; some scholars suspect a Norse influence on the name. It was adopted by the descendants of Gilla Patráic mac Donnchada, king of Osraige from 976 to 996.</p><p>A genealogy of Domnall mac Donnchada mac Gilla Patric, preserved in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodleian-library">Bodleian Library</a> as MS Rawlinson B 502, traces the dynasty back to Óengus Osrithe, who supposedly flourished in the first or second century, and further back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/noah">Noah</a>, which scholars regard as an attachment of the accounts in Genesis to native tradition. Another early Ossorian genealogy is found in the Book of Leinster.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donnchad-mac-gilla-patraic">Donnchad mac Gilla Pátraic</a>, son of the dynasty's namesake, was king of Osraige from 1003 to 1039 and also king of Leinster from 1033 to 1039, and he was the first king to bear the surname. When the ruling chief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-ossory">Upper Ossory</a> submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a>, he took the anglicised name Fitz-Patrick, and most of the clan followed. His successors held the title of Baron Upper Ossory until the seventh baron was attainted on 11 May 1691 and the barony forfeited. In the 18th century John FitzPatrick was created 1st Earl of Upper Ossory, and in the 19th John FitzPatrick, a Liberal politician, became 1st Baron Castletown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Fitzpatrick arms carry a white saltire on a black field, with a lion and dragon crest; some bear the French augmentation of three fleurs-de-lis, a gift of Henri II to the second Baron Upper Ossory while he served as ambassador for Edward VI. Places linked with the family include Jerpoint Abbey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aghaboe">Aghaboe Abbey</a>, Gowran, Grangefertagh, Ballaghmore Castle and Cullahill Castle. In April 2004 a ground-penetrating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radar">radar</a> survey found what were probably the foundations of the twelfth-century cathedral of the diocese of Ossory and a very large structure, possibly a royal Mac Giolla Phádraig palace.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mac Giolla Phádraig dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His descendants, the <b>Mac Giolla Phádraig</b>, briefly held the kingship of Leinster under Donnchad mac Gilla Pátraic in the 1030s.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-osraige">Kingdom of Osraige</a></li></ul></section>
